abstract: >-
  Simulation code, derived data and figures for a modelling study of cooling
  architecture selection for hyperscale AI data centres at high altitude, where
  atmospheric pressure is only 70-80 kPa and air density falls to about 80% of its
  sea-level value. The model couples altitude to air-side cooling capacity, fan power
  and the air-side coefficient of performance through the air density ratio, giving PUE
  as an explicit function of the liquid-cooling fraction, and is evaluated with a
  full-year hourly simulation driven by measured TMYx weather for Xining (2,266 m),
  Beijing and Shanghai. Version 1.1 adds a GPU training-load layer (compute phase plus
  communication phase) and a three-way parameter sweep over synchronisation interval,
  liquid-cooling fraction and grid carbon intensity. It reports a makespan-versus-carbon
  trade-off curve and shows that mean PUE is identical (1.1490) for ten schedules of the
  same job whose carbon footprint differs by 13x, while the entire liquid-cooling range
  moves carbon by a schedule-independent 9.1%.
